{title=Original phase patterns for the four one-dimensional carbon-ring symmetry orbitals in the printed numbering and axes}

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the four supplied opposite-site combinations in the order $(\phi_1,\phi_2,\phi_3,\phi_4)$. Their <Hückel method> <matrix> is
$$
H_{E_g}=\begin{pmatrix}
\alpha&\beta_S&0&-\beta_L\\
\beta_S&\alpha&\beta_L&0\\
0&\beta_L&\alpha&\beta_S\\
-\beta_L&0&\beta_S&\alpha
\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The negative corner is essential. For example, $\langle\phi_4|H|\phi_1\rangle$ includes the long bonds $4$–$5$ and $8$–$1$, each with opposite coefficient signs, giving $-\beta_L$.

Because the basis is orthonormal, the secular <determinant> is
$$
\boxed{\begin{vmatrix}
\alpha-E&\beta_S&0&-\beta_L\\
\beta_S&\alpha-E&\beta_L&0\\
0&\beta_L&\alpha-E&\beta_S\\
-\beta_L&0&\beta_S&\alpha-E
\end{vmatrix}=0.}
$$
It factors as $\big[(\alpha-E)^2-\beta_S^2-\beta_L^2\big]^2=0$, giving two doubly degenerate <energies>, $\alpha\pm\sqrt{\beta_S^2+\beta_L^2}$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

At $\beta_L=0$, the preceding <matrix> separates into identical $(\phi_1,\phi_2)$ and $(\phi_3,\phi_4)$ blocks. For $\beta_S<0$, a normalized pair at the lower <energy> $\alpha+\beta_S$ is
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\psi_{-,1}&=\frac{\phi_1+\phi_2}{\sqrt2}
=\frac{p_1+p_2-p_5-p_6}{2},\\
\psi_{-,2}&=\frac{\phi_3+\phi_4}{\sqrt2}
=\frac{p_3+p_4-p_7-p_8}{2}.
\end{aligned}}
$$
A pair at the upper <energy> $\alpha-\beta_S$ is
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\psi_{+,1}&=\frac{\phi_1-\phi_2}{\sqrt2}
=\frac{p_1-p_2-p_5+p_6}{2},\\
\psi_{+,2}&=\frac{\phi_3-\phi_4}{\sqrt2}
=\frac{p_3-p_4-p_7+p_8}{2}.
\end{aligned}}
$$
The four coefficient vectors are mutually orthonormal. Any orthogonal mixing within either equal-energy pair is an equally valid choice of <molecular orbitals>. At this limiting parameter value, additional coincidences with the one-dimensional levels occur: the full eight-orbital system is four isolated dimers, with four bonding and four antibonding levels.

